title: Setup guide
---
body:

This guide gets you ready to run the course Marimo notebooks in VS Code using the official marimo extension, GitHub Copilot, and a reproducible **pixi** environment. Additional lecture and assignment notebooks will be published in Marimo format as the course proceeds.

#### Install pixi
- Follow the installation instructions at <https://pixi.sh/latest/>.

#### Install VS Code
- Download and install from <https://code.visualstudio.com/>.

#### Download the course materials
- Direct download (recommended): <https://github.com/yoavram/ModelsPopBiol/archive/refs/heads/master.zip>
  - Unzip and note the folder path (e.g., `~/Downloads/ModelsPopBiol-master`).
- If you prefer `git`, clone <https://github.com/yoavram/ModelsPopBiol> (not covered here).

#### Open the course folder in VS Code
- Start VS Code in the course folder: **File → Open Folder…** → select the course folder.

#### Install VS Code extensions (main workflow)
- Open VS Code → Extensions and install:
  - **Python** (by Microsoft): <https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/python/python-quick-start>
  - **Marimo** (official): <https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=marimo-team.vscode-marimo>
  - **GitHub Copilot**: <https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/setup>
- Get Copilot Pro for free with GitHub Student Pack: <https://education.github.com/pack>

#### Create the course environment from the VS Code terminal
- In VS Code: **Terminal → New Terminal**. Confirm the terminal path is the course folder (the one containing `pixi.lock`).
- Run:
  ```bash
  pixi install --locked
  ```

#### Quick test
In the VS Code terminal, run:
```bash
pixi run python -c "import numpy; print(numpy.__version__)"
```
You should see version number without errors.

#### Open Marimo notebooks in VS Code (main flow)
- Open `index.py` in VS Code.
- Click the Marimo notebook button in the editor to view it as a notebook instead of a `.py` file.

#### Optional: start marimo app from the terminal
- Open one notebook file:
  ```bash
  pixi run marimo edit notebooks/population-growth.py
  ```
- Open a whole folder of notebooks:
  ```bash
  pixi run marimo edit notebooks
  ```
